Digital divide in health access
Social & Environmental
Lack of broadband internet, Inability to navigate patient portals, Missed telehealth appointments

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-025
Reason for Visit: Acute progression of a urinary tract infection to pyelonephritis due to total inability to access the clinic's mandatory digital booking system.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient with severe flank pain, rigors, and high fever who had been trying to see a GP for a week but could not navigate the new online-only booking portal. Urgent assessment was required to diagnose and treat acute pyelonephritis and prevent urosepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back is killing me and I'm shivering so hard my teeth chatter. I tried to see my doctor all week, but the clinic only takes online bookings now and I don't own a computer."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 6-day history of dysuria and urinary frequency, which rapidly progressed over the last 24 hours to severe right-sided back pain, high fevers, and nausea. Key symptoms are directly linked to the digital divide in health access. The patient has a complete lack of broadband internet access at their residence and cannot afford a smartphone. Poor digital literacy resulted in an absolute inability to navigate the patient portal to book an appointment when symptoms were mild. They have missed telehealth opportunities and are entirely excluded from remote patient care, resulting in delayed care and frustration as their local clinic transitioned to a digital-first model.
Functional Status: Normally independent; currently bedbound due to severe pain and rigors
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete disenfranchisement from primary care access, high risk of acute clinical deterioration due to delayed presentation

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 38.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Flushed, diaphoretic, experiencing active rigors, holding right flank
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam is soft with no peritonitis, but there is exquisite, sharp tenderness to percussion over the right costovertebral angle (renal angle tenderness). Cardiovascular exam reveals a hyperdynamic, tachycardic circulation consistent with high fever. Diagnostic tetrad features are evident: complete lack of broadband internet, an inability to navigate patient portals, and a history of missed telehealth/online appointments. Urinalysis reveals 3+ leukocytes, 2+ nitrites, and microscopic hematuria.
Environmental Observations: Older home, landline telephone only, no internet modem or smart devices visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took paracetamol for the fever with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Digital divide in health access (Acute Right Pyelonephritis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Musculoskeletal Back Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high-grade fever, rigors, positive urinalysis, and the specific costovertebral angle tenderness rather than midline spinal pain.
2. Renal Colic (Kidney Stone) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While possible, the presence of a high fever and positive nitrites heavily favors an infectious etiology (pyelonephritis) over an isolated, uncomplicated stone.
3. Lower Urinary Tract Infection (Cystitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The ascension of symptoms, specifically the severe flank pain and systemic pyrexia, indicating upper tract involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation with high fever, rigors, and focal renal angle tenderness indicates acute pyelonephritis, a condition that progressed from a simple cystitis strictly because the patient was digitally excluded from booking a timely primary care appointment. Delaying assessment would have placed the patient at imminent risk of urosepsis, septic shock, and potential acute kidney injury. The objective findings of pyrexia (38.9°C), tachycardia, and infected urine completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to initiate potent, immediate antibiotic therapy and prevent a critical hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of the first dose of oral broad-spectrum antibiotics (e.g., Ciprofloxacin or Cephalexin depending on local guidelines). Administration of oral antipyretics/analgesia (Ibuprofen/Paracetamol).
Positive Behavioral Change: By calling the clinic directly and firmly stating you require a "non-digital access exemption" for your bookings, you bypass the computer system, ensuring you can always speak to a receptionist and get the timely care you need.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Formal letter written by the attending clinician to the patient's regular GP advocating for a flag on the patient's file to allow direct phone bookings.
DME/Safety (Optional): None
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to assess response to antibiotics. The clinician will phone the clinic the following morning to manually secure this appointment for the patient.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes confused, lethargic, unable to keep fluids down due to vomiting, or if the fever does not break within 24 hours of starting antibiotics.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The severe back pain and fever will begin to resolve within 48-72 hours of starting the antibiotics. It is critical to finish the entire course to prevent the kidney infection from returning.
